Velvet Rigging

Creative Catalyst: Two rival circus families wed in a ruined cathedral, forcing their discordant aesthetics to fuse into a singular, haunting wedding march.

Velvet Rigging is the sound of a carousel catching fire in a downpour. It strips the pomp from symphonic metal, replacing the sweeping, heroic orchestrations with the grease-stained, distorted resentment of grunge. The result is "industrial nostalgia"—a genre that feels like mourning a lost childhood while simultaneously sharpening a blade. The percussion is unnervingly massive, utilizing GetGood Drums to simulate the sound of an elephant’s footstep on hollow timber.

Genre DNA
*   Tempo & Pulse: 65–88 BPM, often dragging behind the beat like a heavy, sodden velvet curtain.
*   Harmonic Palette: Aeolian and Phrygian modes played on detuned cellos and baritone guitars; frequent use of dissonant diminished chords that never resolve.
*   Production Signatures: 
    1. Found-Object Reverb: All orchestral elements are passed through a convolution reverb profile captured inside an empty, pressurized circus tent.
    2. Tape-Mangled Heaviness: Mastering via damaged quarter-inch tape to introduce "warble" into the crushing, massive drum transients.
    3. The Anti-Symphony: Orchestral tracks are recorded and then played back at half-speed through a blown-out guitar amplifier.
*   Dynamics: A slow, tectonic build-up of bass-heavy sludge that suddenly collapses into fragile, solo-instrument elegies before re-erupting into claustrophobic walls of sound.

Scene & Origin Lore
The subculture of "Riggers" emerged from the dust of the Zampano and Moretti circus clans. They dress in tattered, vintage Victorian formalwear stained with stage makeup and diesel oil. Their ritual gatherings, known as "The Unravelings," involve lighting a central bonfire and circling it in complete silence until the first, massive drum hit triggers a collective, guttural howl. The fashion is defined by heavy wool tailoring that has been deliberately frayed, accented by antique copper hardware salvaged from collapsed big-tops.

Instruments: Detuned 7-string baritone guitars, cello ensembles fed through fuzz-pedals, GetGood Drums (sampled to sound like impact-heavy hydraulic presses), and an antique upright piano that has been intentionally mistuned.

For Fans Of: Katatonia, Type O Negative, The Smashing Pumpkins, Lingua Ignota.

Fictional Band Name: The Seamstress’s Grudge.

Band Description: A quartet of former aerialists turned gloom-rockers. Lead vocalist Elara "The Knot" Vance sings with a strained, breathy baritone that threatens to crack into a scream, while her bandmates prioritize sustain over speed. They are notorious for performing behind a thin gauze curtain, projecting shaky, black-and-white home movies of circus life onto the fabric as they play.

Band Visual & Press Photoshoot Prompt: A cinematic, wide-angle shot of four figures in decadent, mud-splattered funeral attire standing in a flooded, abandoned circus ring at twilight. The lead singer stands center, holding a frayed silver thread that seems to disappear into a storm cloud above, while massive, rusted iron hoops hang suspended in the background, backlit by a dying, copper-hued sun. High-contrast, gritty, hyper-realistic, dark romanticism.

